WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.855
Task statement 2.1.

2
00:00:01.855 --> 00:00:06.990
Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries.

3
00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:15.263
In a support agent with a dozen MCP tools, one vague description can send refund requests to the wrong tool all day.

4
00:00:16.663 --> 00:00:17.996
Here is the core idea.

5
00:00:17.996 --> 00:00:23.331
Claude never sees your code, only the tool's name, its description and its input schema.

6
00:00:23.331 --> 00:00:25.938
The schema tells Claude how to call a tool.

7
00:00:25.938 --> 00:00:28.666
The description tells Claude when to call it.

8
00:00:28.666 --> 00:00:35.820
So if two drawers in the workshop carry almost the same label, expect the wrong one to be opened, and expect it often.

9
00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:41.568
A strong description reads like the note you would leave for a new teammate.

10
00:00:41.568 --> 00:00:48.605
Say what the tool does, what each input means, show a tricky format, name the limits, and say when to use a different tool.

11
00:00:48.605 --> 00:00:57.529
Anthropic's tool docs call description quality the most important factor in tool performance, and suggest three or four sentences as a floor, not a ceiling.

12
00:00:58.929 --> 00:01:01.796
When two tools sound alike, Claude has to guess.

13
00:01:01.796 --> 00:01:09.500
The fix is to make each tool's job distinct, by renaming, splitting, or rewriting the descriptions so their boundaries are clear.

14
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.232
Also read your system prompt.

15
00:01:11.232 --> 00:01:19.473
A line like always check the order first can pull Claude toward the order tool even when the customer asked about something else entirely.

16
00:01:20.873 --> 00:01:22.665
Picture the support scenario.

17
00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:27.114
Two tools both say gets customer data, and the agent flips between them.

18
00:01:27.114 --> 00:01:29.400
Do not reach for a routing layer yet.

19
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:37.988
First decide what each tool really does, then rewrite the descriptions so each one says what it covers and where the other tool takes over.

20
00:01:37.988 --> 00:01:42.313
Then replay real conversations and measure whether selection improved.

21
00:01:43.713 --> 00:01:46.999
The exam loves the tempting fix that adds machinery.

22
00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:53.318
If tools are misrouted, the right first move is almost always to improve the descriptions and names.

23
00:01:53.318 --> 00:01:58.878
Extra classifiers, louder system prompt rules and catch-all tools all treat the symptom.

24
00:01:58.878 --> 00:02:03.617
A clear, bounded description treats the cause, and it costs almost nothing.

25
00:02:05.017 --> 00:02:05.582
To recap.

26
00:02:05.582 --> 00:02:10.411
Descriptions are how Claude picks tools, so write them like onboarding notes.

27
00:02:10.411 --> 00:02:19.002
Make every tool's boundary clear, rename or split anything that overlaps, and keep an eye on system prompt wording that tilts the choice.

28
00:02:19.002 --> 00:02:23.705
When selection goes wrong, fix the description before you add anything new.

